<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Chronic post-traumatic back, pelvic, and right leg pain after old spinal and pelvic fractures, spinal surgery, and childhood polio, in a 37-year-old painter on long-term opioid therapy. Please design a multimodal rehabilitation plan, review bracing and orthotics, including a shoe lift for her leg-length difference, and set function goals that can guide a slow opioid taper. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Routine, within two weeks. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>What exercise and physical therapy program best fits her spine, pelvis, polio-affected leg, and ankle?</item><item>Would a shoe lift, ankle bracing, or a change in how she uses her back brace help?</item><item>Do any findings call for imaging, nerve testing, or an interventional procedure?</item><item>What function goals should we use to judge progress as the opioid dose is slowly reduced?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Kahlo is seen at the request of Dr. Ortega for chronic post-traumatic pain. She describes constant low back and pelvic pain with burning in the right leg, a little less at night since she started duloxetine two weeks ago, and right ankle pain on standing that the diclofenac gel eases. She can stand at the easel for about 15 minutes before she has to sit, and she paints mostly seated or reclining. She walks short distances with a cane, uses a wheelchair for longer outings, and wears her back brace on bad days. She has had no falls, no new weakness or numbness, and no change in bladder or bowel control. She takes no more than four oxycodone tablets a day. She knows a slow opioid taper is planned and asks that it stay slow. Her own goals are to stand longer at the easel and to keep teaching and painting every day. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Magdalena Carmen Frida Kahlo Calderón was born on July 6, 1907, in her parents&apos; house in Coyoacán, Mexico City, now known as the Blue House, the daughter of Guillermo Kahlo and Matilde Calderón. [D]</item><item>In 1913, at the age of six, Kahlo fell ill with polio; afterward her right leg was thinner, slightly shorter, and less developed, with the foot turned outward, and her father encouraged cycling and swimming. [D]</item><item>A neurologist&apos;s retrospective review proposes that Kahlo also had a congenital spinal anomaly (spina bifida). [R]</item><item>On September 17, 1925, at 18, Kahlo was riding a bus that was struck by a streetcar; a metal handrail impaled her through the hip and fractured her pelvis, and she had three fractures of the spinal column, a fractured collarbone, two rib fractures, a dislocated right shoulder, and eleven fractures of the right leg with a dislocated foot. [D]</item><item>While immobilized for several months after the accident, Kahlo began to paint, using an easel adapted for painting in bed. [D]</item><item>Kahlo lived with chronic pain for the rest of her life, underwent many operations, some with long convalescences and serious consequences, and used about 25 different corsets. [D]</item><item>In 1944, because of increasingly limited mobility, Kahlo had to stop attending her classes at the La Esmeralda school of painting and sculpture, and some students continued to work with her at the Blue House. [D]</item><item>Her pain has been interpreted retrospectively as including chronic neuropathic pain and central sensitization. [R]</item><item>Kahlo&apos;s father, Guillermo Kahlo, had epilepsy and died in 1941; her mother, Matilde Calderón, died in 1932. [D]</item></list><paragraph>3 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

