<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Chronic post-traumatic low back and pelvic pain with right leg neuropathic features (G89.21). Mechanical pain from the old spinal and pelvic injuries and surgery, made worse by the uneven gait, with burning in the right leg that is neuropathic in character. There are no red flags and no new deficit, so imaging would not change management. Active rehabilitation is the main treatment, with duloxetine for the neuropathic component. [S]</item><item>Right leg weakness and shortening after childhood polio (B91). The shorter, weaker right leg tilts the pelvis and loads the back and the right ankle, and a shoe lift should ease both. Strengthening of the weak leg must be paced to avoid overuse fatigue. [S]</item><item>Post-traumatic osteoarthritis of the right ankle and foot (M19.171). Limits standing; topical diclofenac helps and there is no effusion. No injection or surgical opinion is needed now. [S]</item><item>Long-term opioid therapy (Z79.891). Rehabilitation, duloxetine, and pain psychology give her other ways to control pain and support the slow, collaborative taper Dr. Ortega has planned. Opioid prescribing stays with Dr. Ortega alone. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: The right ankle radiograph from the first visit (joint-space narrowing, osteophytes, and healed old fractures); the intake ALT, creatinine, and 25-hydroxyvitamin D; the intake PEG and PHQ-9; and today&apos;s PEG. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Physical therapy twice weekly from today: graded exercise, core and hip strengthening, and aquatic therapy, with a daily home program. Pace the right leg work with rest between sets and no exercise to exhaustion. [S]</item><item>Shoe lift for the shorter right leg, prescribed today, worn in the shoes she stands and walks in. [S]</item><item>Keep the back brace for bad days only, not all day, so the trunk muscles do not weaken; check the skin under it. [S]</item><item>Continue the cane, and the wheelchair for longer outings for now. No ankle brace is needed at present. [S]</item><item>Function goal, chosen by her: stand at the easel for 30 minutes at a time by the six-week review, alternating sitting and standing as she builds up. [S]</item><item>Continue duloxetine, increasing to 60 mg daily as Dr. Ortega plans, scheduled acetaminophen up to 3 g a day, and diclofenac gel for the ankle; no oral anti-inflammatory. [S]</item><item>Cognitive behavioral therapy for chronic pain, with pain neuroscience education in physical therapy. [S]</item><item>No spinal imaging, nerve conduction studies, or injections now; reconsider them for new weakness, a change in bladder or bowel control, or failure to progress with rehabilitation. [S]</item><item>Support a slow opioid taper of about 10 percent a month, starting at the four-week visit and paused if function worsens; judge progress by the PEG and easel time rather than by pain alone. [S]</item><item>The plan was agreed with Ms. Kahlo, who chose the easel goal, and discussed with Dr. Ortega, who sees her today. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Physical therapy will report progress to Dr. Ortega, who carries the plan forward with the PEG at each visit. Rehabilitation will see her again if progress stalls, if new neurological symptoms appear, or if bracing needs to change. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
